; workload-inspect — an SELinux domain for the per-workload egress inspector.
;
; WHY THIS WAS NOT AN EXTENSION OF workload-proxy.cil
;
; Recorded because the module it declined to extend is now deleted, and the
; reasoning is what says whether this one should have absorbed anything from it.
; wlproxy_t was defined by two things the inspector does not share: the binary
; it entrypointed (/usr/bin/tinyproxy) and a filesystem boundary drawn at
; /run/workload-vm/<name>. The inspector is a Python script in
; /usr/libexec/workloadctl and its sockets are opened by init, so nothing about
; the shape transferred. Rung 2 deleted wlproxy_t entirely; nothing moved here,
; because there was nothing in it that this domain lacked.
;
; THE ENTRYPOINT IS A SCRIPT, AND THAT WORKS
;
; Retyping the interpreter is out of the question — /usr/bin/python3 is every
; workloadctl entrypoint and most of the host besides. Labelling the *script* is
; the answer, and shipped Fedora policy already runs that shape:
; /usr/libexec/openssh/sshd-keygen is a #!/usr/bin/bash script carrying
; sshd_keygen_exec_t, with both halves present — a type_transition on the
; script's own label and an entrypoint allow on it.
;
; The analogy stops there, and this module got it wrong until 2026-08-25. It
; claimed "what the domain needs from it is execute_no_trans", copied from
; sshd_keygen_t. But execute_no_trans is only checked when an exec does NOT
; cause a domain transition, and sshd-keygen's does not while ours does — the
; type_transition two paragraphs down is the difference. Measured under a
; permissive harvest, the domain uses `execute map` on bin_t and never
; execute_no_trans, and the base_ro_file_type attribute it was granted on is
; used for nothing at all. Reading the stdlib is unaffected: base policy's
; `allow domain base_ro_file_type:file { getattr ioctl lock open read }` and
; lib_t's own execute/map already cover it.
;
; ONE FILE, NOT A GLOB
;
; The filecon names a single path. /usr/libexec/workloadctl holds the helpers
; too, and workload-vm-inspect runs *privileged* from the socket unit's
; ExecStartPre=+. A glob over that directory would make every helper an
; entrypoint into this domain — the same class of silent over-reach as the
; /usr/sbin symlink trap workload-proxy.cil documents, and in the same
; direction, since nothing would fail. Everything under libexec/ is bin_t
; today, and bin_t carries both exec_type and entry_type, which is exactly why
; the retype is necessary rather than a bin_t:file entrypoint.
;
; SOCKET ACTIVATION DOES NOT MOVE THE BIND OUT OF THE DOMAIN
;
; The design predicted it would: the sockets are opened by init, so the domain
; should need no port label at all. Measured, it needs two. systemd's
; (sd-listen) both CREATES and BINDS a socket unit's sockets in the *service's*
; domain, so the denials are
;
;   avc: denied { create } scontext=init_t tcontext=wlinspect_t
;   avc: denied { name_bind } scontext=wlinspect_t tcontext=http_cache_port_t
;
; — one rule pointing each way, and neither is the "inherit an init_t socket"
; rule that was expected. Both surface as the socket unit failing to start,
; taking the VM ordered after it down with a bare dependency error.
;
; A THIRD RULE IS DONTAUDIT'D, AND A PLAIN ENFORCING HARVEST CANNOT SEE IT
;
; systemd creates the socket in a forked (sd-listen) helper and passes the fd
; back to PID 1 over a socketpair, so init_t also needs `read write` on the
; wlinspect_t socket. That denial is dontaudit'd in shipped policy: with it
; missing the audit log is EMPTY and the unit fails with
;
;   Failed to receive listening socket (198.18.1.1:8080):
;     Channel number out of range
;
; which is receive_one_fd() reporting -ECHRNG for a control message that
; carried no descriptor. Nothing in that names SELinux. It was found by
; running `semodule -DB` to disable dontaudit rules and starting the unit
; again; re-derive it that way, because four enforcing iterations without
; that step converge on a module that is still one rule short.
;
; THE TWO PLANES CARRY DIFFERENT PORT LABELS
;
; Read from `semanage port -l` rather than guessed, which is why
; workload-proxy.cil's squid_port_t comment exists:
;
;   8080 (cleartext) -> http_cache_port_t
;   8443 (tls)       -> http_port_t
;
; Granting one and not the other yields a half-working inspector — one plane
; binds, the other does not — and the plane that fails is whichever the reader
; did not think about. Both, or the pair is wrong.
;
; HARVESTING
;
; Derived by enforcing iterations against a real guest dialling the listener,
; not by a single permissive pass. A permissive run gets further here than it
; did for the retired tinyproxy domain — Python reaches its imports and its
; inherited socket before it needs anything else, where tinyproxy exited 70 at
; the first gate — but the accept path is still a gate behind a
; gate, so the last rules only appear once the earlier ones are granted.
; tests/manual/inspect_rig.py is the run that exercises it.
;
; SIX RESIDUAL DENIALS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT GRANTED
;
; A green run with dontaudit disabled still logs these, and the run is green
; WITH them denied — so each would widen the domain for nothing. The list was
; four until a permissive harvest on 2026-08-25 measured the full used-set;
; two more were being logged all along, and the cert_t entry understated what
; is attempted:
;
;   getattr, ioctl(TCGETS) on init_t:unix_stream_socket   Python probing
;     whether its stdout is a tty. The answer is "no" either way.
;   getattr, open, read, search on cert_t (/etc/pki)      import machinery.
;     NOT merely `search`: the domain attempts to open and read files under
;     it. Still denied, still green — the listener splices TLS and never
;     terminates it, so it has no use for a CA bundle.
;   search on container_file_t (/var/lib/workloads)       walking past the
;     workload state tree it must not read
;   search on svirt_image_t                               likewise, the
;     guest's own disks
;
; init_var_run_t:dir search was in this list for about an hour and does NOT
; belong: it is granted above, because the resolver traverses /run/systemd to
; reach the stub resolv.conf. It measured as "used, and the run is green
; anyway" only because no green run had ever resolved a hostname.
;
; The last two are the boundary working. wlproxy_t is a separate domain from
; svirt_t precisely so that the component terminating guest input cannot reach
; the workload's disks and state; granting `search` here because it appears in
; a log would be the first step back across that line, and nothing would fail
; to tell anyone. Re-check them if the listener ever gains a reason to read a
; certificate — §6 puts the CA in a subdirectory with its OWN label for this
; reason, and that is the rule to add then, not these.

(type wlinspect_t)
(typeattributeset domain (wlinspect_t))
(roletype system_r wlinspect_t)

; roletype before the filecon, or semodule rejects the module with
; "Type wlinspect_exec_t is invalid for role object_r".
(type wlinspect_exec_t)
(roletype object_r wlinspect_exec_t)
(typeattributeset file_type (wlinspect_exec_t))
(typeattributeset exec_type (wlinspect_exec_t))
(typeattributeset entry_type (wlinspect_exec_t))
(filecon "/usr/libexec/workloadctl/workload-vm-inspect-listener" file (system_u object_r wlinspect_exec_t ((s0) (s0))))

; Straight out of systemd, like wlproxy_t and unlike QEMU: the module declares
; the transition itself, so the unit's ExecStart is the plain script path.
(typetransition init_t wlinspect_exec_t process wlinspect_t)
(allow init_t wlinspect_exec_t (file (getattr open read execute map)))
(allow init_t wlinspect_t (process (transition siginh)))
(allow wlinspect_t wlinspect_exec_t (file (entrypoint getattr ioctl open read)))

; The interpreter. execute_no_trans is granted on the attribute, not on a
; literal bin_t: shipped policy grants sshd_keygen_t the same permission on
; base_ro_file_type, of which bin_t (/usr/bin/python3.14) and lib_t are both
; members. Naming bin_t alone would work for the interpreter and miss
; everything else the attribute covers.
;
; Reading the stdlib is NOT an addition: `allow domain base_ro_file_type:file
; { getattr ioctl lock open read }` already applies to every domain, and
; /usr/lib64/python3.14/** is lib_t. Bytecode writes stay absent because every
; entrypoint sets sys.dont_write_bytecode.
(allow wlinspect_t bin_t (file (execute map)))

; stdout/stderr to the journal — the log line per connection is the whole of
; this rung's output.
(allow wlinspect_t init_t (unix_stream_socket (read write)))

; The inherited listening sockets, and this is the rule the design guessed
; wrong. The guess was that the fds carry init_t and the domain needs
; `wlinspect_t init_t:tcp_socket accept read write`. Measured: systemd creates
; a socket unit's sockets ALREADY LABELLED for the service's domain, so the
; denial is on the other side of the arrow —
;
;   avc: denied { create } comm="(sd-listen)"
;     scontext=…init_t tcontext=…wlinspect_t tclass=tcp_socket
;
; — init_t creating a wlinspect_t socket. Nothing about it is visible from the
; inspector's side: the process never starts, the socket unit fails, and the
; VM that is ordered after it fails with a bare dependency error. The domain
; then uses those same sockets as `self`, which is the rule below.
(allow init_t wlinspect_t (tcp_socket (bind create getattr getopt listen read setopt shutdown write)))

; The two listener ports, both labels, for the reason in the header. node_bind
; covers binding to the workload's own listener address rather than a wildcard.
(allow wlinspect_t http_cache_port_t (tcp_socket (name_bind)))
(allow wlinspect_t http_port_t (tcp_socket (name_bind name_connect)))
(allow wlinspect_t node_t (tcp_socket (node_bind)))

; THE UPSTREAM DIAL, WHICH THREE GREEN RIG RUNS NEVER EXERCISED
;
; Everything above is the LISTEN side. The rules below are the other half --
; the inspector opening its own connection to the allowlisted host -- and they
; were absent from this module until 2026-08-25 because nothing had ever run
; that path. The rig's every event was a drop: {"dropped": 5, "forwarded": 0,
; "spliced": 0} on a 31/31 run. The plain arm's allowlist is empty by
; construction, and the proxy arm's traffic is exempted by wl_inspect_cg before
; the inspector sees it, so forward and splice were dead code under SELinux
; while the suite reported green.
;
; The path is reached by a guest that IGNORES the proxy variables and dials an
; allowlisted host directly -- not a corner case but the adversarial case this
; whole rung exists for, per lib/vm.py: "the drop is what makes the allowlist
; binding". A cooperating guest proves nothing about it.
;
; WHY IT WAS SILENT, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH REMEMBERING
;
; A denial here does not crash the inspector and does not appear as a failure.
; connect() or getaddrinfo() raises, the relay catches OSError, and the
; connection is counted as a DROP with reason 'upstream unreachable'. That is
; byte-identical to a genuinely dead upstream. Measured under enforcing with
; these rules missing: 502 to the guest, and
;   drop plane=cleartext host=example.com
;     reason='upstream unreachable: [Errno -3] Temporary failure in name resolution'
; -- a policy gap wearing a network error's clothes. No counter distinguishes
; them, so do not read a clean drop total as evidence this side works.
;
; Resolution fails FIRST, before any socket is created, which is why the
; resolv.conf rules matter as much as the socket ones: net_conf_t is
; /etc/resolv.conf (a symlink to the stub resolver's copy on this host, hence
; lnk_file). getaddrinfo then dials the stub over UDP, which needs a socket the
; domain creates itself rather than one inherited from init.
; The resolver's path, and it is three labels deep rather than one. Read from
; the host rather than assumed:
;
;   /etc/resolv.conf                          net_conf_t (lnk_file)
;     -> ../run/systemd                       init_var_run_t (dir)
;        -> resolve/                          systemd_resolved_var_run_t (dir)
;           -> stub-resolv.conf               net_conf_t (file)
;
; Every dir in that chain needs `search` or the read fails at the traversal,
; not at the file. The init_var_run_t entry was briefly filed as a harmless
; residual denial on the strength of a permissive harvest showing it "used but
; the run was green" -- wrong, because the run was green only on paths that
; never resolved a hostname.
;
; `setopt` is here for a sharper reason: it does NOT appear in a permissive
; harvest of this path. It only showed up under enforcing, once the traversal
; above was granted. A permissive run yields the used-set of the branch that
; actually ran, and an earlier denial changes which branch glibc takes -- so a
; harvest is one sample, not a closed-form answer. Iterate under enforcing
; until the log is quiet; do not treat the first harvest as complete.
(allow wlinspect_t self (udp_socket (connect create getattr setopt)))
(allow wlinspect_t net_conf_t (file (getattr open read)))
(allow wlinspect_t net_conf_t (lnk_file (read)))
(allow wlinspect_t init_var_run_t (dir (search)))
(allow wlinspect_t systemd_resolved_var_run_t (dir (search)))

; The TCP half of the same lookup, and it is here by argument rather than by
; harvest -- no run of ours has produced a truncated answer to force it. A UDP
; response that does not fit sets TC, and glibc then retries the whole query
; over TCP to port 53; without this the retry is denied and the caller sees
; EACCES from connect. By this module's own standard that surfaces as
; `drop ... reason='upstream unreachable'` -- a policy gap wearing a network
; error's clothes, and one that appears only for the names whose answers are
; large (a CDN with many A records, a host behind a long CNAME chain), which is
; to say intermittently and for someone else's names.
;
; Granted rather than ruled out, because ruling it out means asserting that no
; name any operator ever allowlists has a truncating answer, which is not ours
; to assert. It widens nothing the UDP grant did not already: the destination
; is the same port on the same resolver.
(allow wlinspect_t dns_port_t (tcp_socket (name_connect)))

; And a third thing getaddrinfo does that neither the harvest nor the first two
; enforcing runs could show: before it returns, glibc's __check_pf opens a
; NETLINK_ROUTE socket to enumerate the host's own addresses, so it can order
; the A/AAAA results by source-address selection (RFC 3484). This denial was
; masked twice over -- by the resolv.conf traversal, and then by udp setopt --
; and surfaced only once both were granted. It is also non-fatal: glibc falls
; back and resolution still succeeds, so the rig stayed green on the probes and
; failed only on its own audit-log assertion. Without that assertion the gap
; would have shipped looking perfect.
;
; All six permissions are measured, not inferred. `create` was the only one the
; enforcing run could show -- a denial there ends the sequence -- so the set was
; first written from glibc's __check_pf call shape and then confirmed by a
; harvest against the corrected policy: bind, create, getattr, nlmsg_read, read
; and write all appear, and nothing else does.
(allow wlinspect_t self (netlink_route_socket (bind create getattr nlmsg_read read write)))

; The listening sockets as the domain sees them, plus the accepted connections,
; which carry the same label.
(allow wlinspect_t self (tcp_socket (accept connect create getattr getopt read write)))

; Its own runtime directory: /run/workload-vm/<name>, which workload-vm.cil's
; sibling arrangement labels qemu_var_run_t. Two distinct uses, and they were
; added at different times for different reasons — worth keeping straight,
; because granting for one and not the other fails in two unrelated ways.
;
;   inspect.json         READ, at start. The policy document: allowlists, the
;                        internal list, the TLS settings. Without this the
;                        process exits 1 before it accepts anything, the socket
;                        unit restart-loops, and every guest dial to 80/443
;                        times out with nothing in the guest to say why. LOUD:
;                        the journal names the file and the errno.
;   inspect-status.json  WRITTEN, periodically, via a temp file and os.replace.
;                        The counters rung 5 reads. SILENT: the writer catches
;                        OSError and logs a warning by design, so a missing
;                        grant here costs the file and nothing else — a green
;                        rig, a working inspector, and no observability at all.
;
; `rename` is the one permission no sibling grant carries, and it is not
; optional — asserted here from a reading of write_status() when this module
; was written, and MEASURED on 2026-08-25 under a permissive harvest, which
; recorded the file class as used: create getattr open read rename unlink
; write. `unlink` is in that set for a reason worth keeping: os.replace over an
; existing target needs it on the destination, so it is load-bearing only from
; the SECOND status write onward. A harvest that ticks once misses it; the
; rig's STATUS_SETTLE against STATUS_INTERVAL is what makes it appear.
; The same harvest trimmed `lock` and `setattr` from this line, and getattr,
; open and read from the dir line below, as used by nothing. wlproxy_t writes its log and pid file in place and never renames;
; the status write is a temp-file-plus-os.replace so that a reader never sees a
; half-written document. Copying wlproxy_t's file line verbatim therefore
; produces a grant that gets all the way to the replace and fails there — the
; silent half of the table above, reached by a route that looks correct.
(allow wlinspect_t qemu_var_run_t (dir (add_name remove_name search write)))
(allow wlinspect_t qemu_var_run_t (file (create getattr open read rename unlink write)))
